\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The Agent Studio. The seven-step tab at the top (\emph  {Identity, State \& Personality, Abilities \& Skills, Memory, Social \& Relationships, Behavior \& Goals, Review \& Deploy}) maps one to one onto the seven sections of an Agent Studio session. The left column selects the city and the resident; the right radar plots the normalized state across nine axes (activity, public-expression, pressure, economic sense, city recognition, policy sensitivity, risk preference, platform dependence, mobility intent). The \emph  {Profile} pane is the sourced-portrait view; the persona distillation module can write a similar portrait from a name or a URL through the \emph  {Distill a real person} button on the top right.}}{33}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:agent-studio}{{3}{33}{The Agent Studio. The seven-step tab at the top (\emph {Identity, State \& Personality, Abilities \& Skills, Memory, Social \& Relationships, Behavior \& Goals, Review \& Deploy}) maps one to one onto the seven sections of an Agent Studio session. The left column selects the city and the resident; the right radar plots the normalized state across nine axes (activity, public-expression, pressure, economic sense, city recognition, policy sensitivity, risk preference, platform dependence, mobility intent). The \emph {Profile} pane is the sourced-portrait view; the persona distillation module can write a similar portrait from a name or a URL through the \emph {Distill a real person} button on the top right}{figure.3}{}}
\newlabel{fig:agent-studio@cref}{{[figure][3][]3}{[1][7][]33}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Audited v2 execution architecture. The kernel (Clock, EventBus, PluginRegistry, Controller, Recorder, SimContext) serves the cognition pipeline and the per-tenant world resolver. Fourteen built-ins contribute through the registry, bus, pipeline filters, or lifecycle events. Movement uses the Controller validation gate; Recorder events coexist with established domain-specific outputs. In a multi-tenant deployment, the world resolver is the request-time entry point; in a multi-node deployment, the hub routes player interventions to the owning node.}}{34}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:system-architecture}{{4}{34}{Audited v2 execution architecture. The kernel (Clock, EventBus, PluginRegistry, Controller, Recorder, SimContext) serves the cognition pipeline and the per-tenant world resolver. Fourteen built-ins contribute through the registry, bus, pipeline filters, or lifecycle events. Movement uses the Controller validation gate; Recorder events coexist with established domain-specific outputs. In a multi-tenant deployment, the world resolver is the request-time entry point; in a multi-node deployment, the hub routes player interventions to the owning node}{figure.4}{}}
\newlabel{fig:system-architecture@cref}{{[figure][4][]4}{[1][9][]34}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces The fourteen v2 built-in plugins and their relationship to the kernel. Plugins may subscribe to lifecycle events, contribute to perception, filter a value, or own namespaced state. The v1 plugin set (nine) is shown above the v2 additions (five) for comparison.}}{34}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:plugin-assembly}{{5}{34}{The fourteen v2 built-in plugins and their relationship to the kernel. Plugins may subscribe to lifecycle events, contribute to perception, filter a value, or own namespaced state. The v1 plugin set (nine) is shown above the v2 additions (five) for comparison}{figure.5}{}}
\newlabel{fig:plugin-assembly@cref}{{[figure][5][]5}{[1][9][]34}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Persistent experience and growth paths. Tick-level episodes feed retrieval and rule-based updates; configured day-boundary processes may consolidate episodes into semantic memories or private skills, classify growth into a phase, drain the life-event queue, and tally governance ballots. Solid storage paths denote implemented persistence, not validated models of human remembering or learning. Dashed paths indicate optional LLM calls that may be disabled in a controlled experiment.}}{35}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:memory-growth}{{6}{35}{Persistent experience and growth paths. Tick-level episodes feed retrieval and rule-based updates; configured day-boundary processes may consolidate episodes into semantic memories or private skills, classify growth into a phase, drain the life-event queue, and tally governance ballots. Solid storage paths denote implemented persistence, not validated models of human remembering or learning. Dashed paths indicate optional LLM calls that may be disabled in a controlled experiment}{figure.6}{}}
\newlabel{fig:memory-growth@cref}{{[figure][6][]6}{[1][11][]35}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Coupling among agent and societal components. Evidence status describes implementation visibility, not empirical validity.}}{36}{table.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:component-coupling}{{3}{36}{Coupling among agent and societal components. Evidence status describes implementation visibility, not empirical validity}{table.3}{}}
\newlabel{tab:component-coupling@cref}{{[table][3][]3}{[1][15][]36}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Request resolution in a multi-tenant deployment. A request enters the dashboard server with a session cookie and a \texttt  {gaworld\_world} cookie; \texttt  {\_guard} authenticates the caller, the \texttt  {WorldResolver} validates the cookie against the caller's role and visibility, and the resulting \texttt  {WorldContext} drives per-tenant path resolution.}}{37}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:multi-tenant}{{7}{37}{Request resolution in a multi-tenant deployment. A request enters the dashboard server with a session cookie and a \texttt {gaworld\_world} cookie; \texttt {\_guard} authenticates the caller, the \texttt {WorldResolver} validates the cookie against the caller's role and visibility, and the resulting \texttt {WorldContext} drives per-tenant path resolution}{figure.7}{}}
\newlabel{fig:multi-tenant@cref}{{[figure][7][]7}{[1][15][]37}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces The Cities panel. A v2 deployment lists every city bundle it has access to; the panel creates a new city from a real place name (geocoded via Nominatim with OpenStreetMap data) or from an imagined description (procedurally generated). Each entry shows the source (\emph  {Real map} or \emph  {Procedural}), the scale (\emph  {tiny / small / medium / large}), and the agent count. Selecting a city loads its bundle into the running world; the \emph  {In use} tag indicates the bundle currently driving the active simulation. This panel is the entry point for per-tenant world isolation: when accounts are enabled, each user has a personal \emph  {In use} pointer and a list of worlds they own or can join.}}{38}{figure.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:cities}{{8}{38}{The Cities panel. A v2 deployment lists every city bundle it has access to; the panel creates a new city from a real place name (geocoded via Nominatim with OpenStreetMap data) or from an imagined description (procedurally generated). Each entry shows the source (\emph {Real map} or \emph {Procedural}), the scale (\emph {tiny / small / medium / large}), and the agent count. Selecting a city loads its bundle into the running world; the \emph {In use} tag indicates the bundle currently driving the active simulation. This panel is the entry point for per-tenant world isolation: when accounts are enabled, each user has a personal \emph {In use} pointer and a list of worlds they own or can join}{figure.8}{}}
\newlabel{fig:cities@cref}{{[figure][8][]8}{[1][15][]38}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Distributed playable topology. The hub is the dashboard server; it owns accounts, the world metadata, the per-node outbox, the per-world relay, and the per-tenant Recorder merge. Each node runs the simulation for its assigned residents, polls interventions, and uploads Recorder rows. All cross-machine traffic is node-to-hub; the hub never opens inbound ports to a node.}}{39}{figure.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:hub-node}{{9}{39}{Distributed playable topology. The hub is the dashboard server; it owns accounts, the world metadata, the per-node outbox, the per-world relay, and the per-tenant Recorder merge. Each node runs the simulation for its assigned residents, polls interventions, and uploads Recorder rows. All cross-machine traffic is node-to-hub; the hub never opens inbound ports to a node}{figure.9}{}}
\newlabel{fig:hub-node@cref}{{[figure][9][]9}{[1][17][]39}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Parallel-worlds engine. Up to eight matched worlds share the same seed, the same resident sample, and the same model. Each world receives a per-world \texttt  {config} patch and a per-world event list; the counterfactual layer then computes paired ATE, sign-flip $p$, pre-event balance, placebo noise floor, heterogeneity, dose--response, and replication. An optional one-call model interpretation binds every claim to a specific evidence row.}}{40}{figure.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:parallel-worlds}{{10}{40}{Parallel-worlds engine. Up to eight matched worlds share the same seed, the same resident sample, and the same model. Each world receives a per-world \texttt {config} patch and a per-world event list; the counterfactual layer then computes paired ATE, sign-flip $p$, pre-event balance, placebo noise floor, heterogeneity, dose--response, and replication. An optional one-call model interpretation binds every claim to a specific evidence row}{figure.10}{}}
\newlabel{fig:parallel-worlds@cref}{{[figure][10][]10}{[1][19][]40}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces The Parallel Worlds tab. A researcher fills in the experiment name, the simulation days, the random seed, the number of seed replicates (for the per-seed ATE and meta-analytic mean), the number of worlds to run in parallel (up to eight), the resident subset, and the model. The \emph  {Fast mode} toggle switches the engine to deterministic cognition with a smaller resident set so that the workflow can be rehearsed cheaply before a real run.}}{41}{figure.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:parallel-tab}{{11}{41}{The Parallel Worlds tab. A researcher fills in the experiment name, the simulation days, the random seed, the number of seed replicates (for the per-seed ATE and meta-analytic mean), the number of worlds to run in parallel (up to eight), the resident subset, and the model. The \emph {Fast mode} toggle switches the engine to deterministic cognition with a smaller resident set so that the workflow can be rehearsed cheaply before a real run}{figure.11}{}}
\newlabel{fig:parallel-tab@cref}{{[figure][11][]11}{[1][19][]41}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces The Research Workbench. A researcher pastes a research idea or uploads a paper, picks a model, and the system returns a plan: a theory, a methods paragraph, key variables, hypotheses, a feature map to the GAWorld surface, two or three experimental designs, and a list of limits. The plan is editable; the model cannot write to a feature that is not in \texttt  {FEATURES.md}, and the protocol compiler cannot use a metric that is not in the \emph  {measurable catalog}. The \emph  {Study plans}, \emph  {Serious games}, \emph  {Policy simulation}, \emph  {Survey / experiment fidelity}, and \emph  {Parallel Worlds Counterfactuals} tabs reuse the same pane to compile plans, run serious-game sessions, and run paper-fidelity studies.}}{42}{figure.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:research-workbench}{{12}{42}{The Research Workbench. A researcher pastes a research idea or uploads a paper, picks a model, and the system returns a plan: a theory, a methods paragraph, key variables, hypotheses, a feature map to the GAWorld surface, two or three experimental designs, and a list of limits. The plan is editable; the model cannot write to a feature that is not in \texttt {FEATURES.md}, and the protocol compiler cannot use a metric that is not in the \emph {measurable catalog}. The \emph {Study plans}, \emph {Serious games}, \emph {Policy simulation}, \emph {Survey / experiment fidelity}, and \emph {Parallel Worlds Counterfactuals} tabs reuse the same pane to compile plans, run serious-game sessions, and run paper-fidelity studies}{figure.12}{}}
\newlabel{fig:research-workbench@cref}{{[figure][12][]12}{[1][19][]42}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces The Analytics pane. After a run, the analytics pane exposes per-run counts (agents, state samples, replay frames, environment events, diary entries, relationships), a per-day event timeline broken down by source (\emph  {social / natural / economic / political / life\_event / technology}), an event-type distribution, and an impact-tag distribution. When a run is incomplete or a metric is missing, the pane displays \emph  {No data yet} in the corresponding block rather than fabricating a value, in line with the evidence-class policy.}}{43}{figure.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:analytics}{{13}{43}{The Analytics pane. After a run, the analytics pane exposes per-run counts (agents, state samples, replay frames, environment events, diary entries, relationships), a per-day event timeline broken down by source (\emph {social / natural / economic / political / life\_event / technology}), an event-type distribution, and an impact-tag distribution. When a run is incomplete or a metric is missing, the pane displays \emph {No data yet} in the corresponding block rather than fabricating a value, in line with the evidence-class policy}{figure.13}{}}
\newlabel{fig:analytics@cref}{{[figure][13][]13}{[1][19][]43}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces The Agent Playground. Each card is a game-based probe; the fourteen cards in the v2 set cover the four families (one-on-one, one-group-one-event, mission / creative, and long-form / human-in-the-loop). Every card is a deterministic harness over a real resident's response; per-game archives are written to \texttt  {output/games/<game>/} on completion and feed the Track B scorecard.}}{44}{figure.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:playground}{{14}{44}{The Agent Playground. Each card is a game-based probe; the fourteen cards in the v2 set cover the four families (one-on-one, one-group-one-event, mission / creative, and long-form / human-in-the-loop). Every card is a deterministic harness over a real resident's response; per-game archives are written to \texttt {output/games/<game>/} on completion and feed the Track B scorecard}{figure.14}{}}
\newlabel{fig:playground@cref}{{[figure][14][]14}{[1][21][]44}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces The Agent Arena. A researcher picks a target city, a panel of up to twelve residents, and a question set (a built-in bank of ten questions or an LLM-generated set). After the round, the leaderboard ranks the residents by correctness and median latency. A \emph  {Keep Top-K} policy retires the bottom of the panel; the \emph  {Refill the panel} controls pull new residents from another city or from a bulk-import file.}}{45}{figure.15}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:arena}{{15}{45}{The Agent Arena. A researcher picks a target city, a panel of up to twelve residents, and a question set (a built-in bank of ten questions or an LLM-generated set). After the round, the leaderboard ranks the residents by correctness and median latency. A \emph {Keep Top-K} policy retires the bottom of the panel; the \emph {Refill the panel} controls pull new residents from another city or from a bulk-import file}{figure.15}{}}
\newlabel{fig:arena@cref}{{[figure][15][]15}{[1][21][]45}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces Coverage of the audited v2 result archive. The matrix distinguishes inspectable capability evidence from incomplete evaluations and diagnostic fixtures; it is not a leaderboard or a validation score.}}{46}{figure.16}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:capability-matrix}{{16}{46}{Coverage of the audited v2 result archive. The matrix distinguishes inspectable capability evidence from incomplete evaluations and diagnostic fixtures; it is not a leaderboard or a validation score}{figure.16}{}}
\newlabel{fig:capability-matrix@cref}{{[figure][16][]16}{[1][23][]46}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Status of all experiment families represented in the v2 artifact ledger. Counts and horizons describe the archived files, not a common evaluation protocol.}}{47}{table.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:archive-status}{{4}{47}{Status of all experiment families represented in the v2 artifact ledger. Counts and horizons describe the archived files, not a common evaluation protocol}{table.4}{}}
